# Getting Started

This Livebook declares one Agent definition, instantiates it as an Agent value,
and runs the value in a live Agent Server. Run each cell in order.

## Install Jido

From this repository, use the local checkout:

```elixir
Mix.install([
  {:jido, path: Path.expand("..", __DIR__)}
])
```

An application can use `{:jido, "~> 3.0.0-beta.1"}` after that beta is
available from Hex.

## Define One Action

An Action gets validated input and Turn context. The current Agent state is in
`context.agent_state`. Return the complete next state.

```elixir
defmodule GettingStarted.Add do
  use Jido.Action,
    name: "getting_started_add",
    schema: Zoi.object(%{amount: Zoi.integer()})

  @impl Jido.Action
  def run(%{amount: amount}, %{agent_state: state}) do
    {:ok, %{state | count: state.count + amount}}
  end
end
```

Jido uses the Action and Flow contracts from
[Jido Action](https://hexdocs.pm/jido_action/). An Action can do I/O. Such I/O
is outside the Agent state commit.

## Declare One Agent

The declaration describes the Agent data schema and the routes from Signals to
executable work. It does not start a process. The nested `define` adds Signal
and live-call helpers.

```elixir
defmodule GettingStarted.Counter do
  use Jido.Agent, name: "getting_started_counter"

  agent do
    schema Zoi.object(%{count: Zoi.integer() |> Zoi.default(0)})
  end

  routes do
    signal_source "/guides/getting-started"

    route "counter.add", GettingStarted.Add do
      defaults %{amount: 1}
      define :add, args: [{:optional, :amount}]
    end
  end
end
```

The module declaration produces a reusable neutral Agent definition. It has no
identity and no live state.

## Apply A Direct Command

`new!/1` instantiates the declared Agent with an identity and initial state. A
direct command returns a new Agent value and its Directives. It does not start a
process or commit live state.

```elixir
agent = GettingStarted.Counter.new!(id: "direct-counter")
{:ok, signal} = GettingStarted.Counter.add_signal(3)
{:ok, candidate, []} = GettingStarted.Counter.cmd(agent, signal)

{agent.state.count, candidate.state.count}
```

The result is `{0, 3}`. The original Agent is unchanged.

## Run The Live Actor

An Agent Server is the live actor for one Agent instance. It accepts one Turn
at a time and commits each valid candidate.

```elixir
{:ok, _jido} = Jido.start()

{:ok, counter} =
  Jido.start_agent(
    GettingStarted.Counter,
    id: "live-counter"
  )

{:ok, committed} = GettingStarted.Counter.add(counter, 3)
snapshot = Jido.AgentServer.snapshot(counter)

{committed.state.count, snapshot.state_version}
```

The result is `{3, 1}`. The Server owns the committed value and its revision.

## Stop The Example

```elixir
:ok = Jido.stop_agent(counter)
:ok = Jido.stop()
```

Continue with [Build Your First Agent](build-your-first-agent.livemd) or read
[Actors, Agents, And Jido](actor-and-agent-framework.md).
